    <div class="help-content">

      <section id="getting-started" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-compass"></i> {{t 'hf.sGettingStarted'}}</h2>
        <p>Aegis SA is a security assessment &amp; authorization (SA&amp;A) platform. You sign in as an
          <b>assessor / administrator</b> or as an invited <b>project member</b>; multi-factor authentication
          (MFA) is available and can be enforced org-wide. After signing in you land on your <b>Dashboard</b>,
          which summarises your projects, active assessments, pending audits, authorizations, intakes and
          pre-assessments.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Administrators</b> see the full workspace and every project in their organization.</li>
          <li><b>Project members</b> see only the projects and assessments they're assigned to.</li>
          <li><b>Language</b> — switch anytime from the globe menu (top right); the whole UI is available in
            8 languages.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="tutorial" class="help-sec">
        <span class="new-badge">Tutorial</span>
        <h2><i class="bi bi-signpost-2"></i> {{t 'hf.sTutorial'}}</h2>
        <p>This end-to-end walkthrough follows one system from its first <b>intake</b> request all the way to
          an <b>authorization decision (ATO/iATO)</b>, and lists every sub-step in order. Each step links to the
          area that performs it — the sections below the walkthrough describe what you can do, what you should
          do, and when, in each area.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-list-ol me-1"></i> The full journey, step by step</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Capture the intake.</b> A requester submits an <b>Intake</b> (or you
            create one on their behalf). Attach any supporting documentation; the Aegis SA Assistant can
            <i>pre-fill</i> the intake from an uploaded document to save typing.</li>
          <li><b>Triage &amp; accept the intake.</b> On <b>Intakes</b>, review the request and <b>Accept</b> it.
            Accepting attaches it to a project <i>in place</i> — it never creates a duplicate assessment.</li>
          <li><b>Create / confirm the project.</b> The <a href="?section=projects">project</a> holds the
            system's ITSG-33 categorization (Confidentiality / Integrity / Availability), security profile and
            branding. Set or confirm the classification here.</li>
          <li><b>Start an assessment.</b> From the project, create an
            <a href="?section=assessments">assessment</a> and choose the framework and profile (e.g. ITSG-33
            PBMM). This generates the tailored control baseline.</li>
          <li><b>Tailor the controls.</b> Mark applicability, inheritance and priority. Optionally tick
            <i>"Add suggested evidence drafts"</i> so every control starts with a fill-in-the-blank draft.</li>
          <li><b>Assign &amp; send.</b> Use <b>Assign &amp; send</b> to assign the assessment to the project
            user — this <i>activates</i> it and emails the invite in one step. The assignee becomes the current
            <b>evidence owner</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Gather evidence</b> (project user). The assignee opens the
            <a href="?section=evidence">evidence flow</a>, writes evidence for each control — typing it, using
            <b>Suggest a draft</b>, asking the assistant to draft and <b>Approve</b> it, or running
            <b>Suggest drafts in bulk</b> — attaches artifacts, replaces every <code>[[VALUE]]</code>/
            <code>[[ATTACH]]</code> placeholder, marks each control <b>Ready</b>, then <b>Submits</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Start the audit.</b> Back on the assessment, <b>Start audit</b> locks the evidence for review.</li>
          <li><b>Review &amp; score.</b> For each control, record the determination (satisfied / partial / not
            satisfied) and rate the evidence's <b>reliability</b>, <b>sufficiency</b> and <b>impact weight</b>;
            leave feedback and set <b>Reviewed</b>, <b>Requires re-submission</b>, or <b>Removed</b>. A
            <b>weighted evidence score</b> and a weak-evidence flag guide (never replace) your judgement.</li>
          <li><b>Return for revision</b> (if needed). <b>Return for revision &amp; re-assign</b> sends the
            assessment back showing <i>only</i> the controls flagged <i>Requires re-submission</i>; the provider
            fixes those and re-submits.</li>
          <li><b>Complete the audit.</b> <b>Complete audit</b> computes the result and proposes
            <b>ATO / iATO / denied</b> — a single unmet <i>high-risk</i> control blocks a full ATO — which you
            can override with justification.</li>
          <li><b>Create the decision package.</b> From the project, create a
            <a href="?section=decisions">Decision Package</a> that pins that assessment version (immutable). For
            an iATO, <a href="?section=poam">POA&amp;M</a> items become its conditions.</li>
          <li><b>Authorize (ATO/iATO).</b> Record the decision, signatures and expiry, and export the
            authorization report (PDF/DOCX). The project's process flow now shows the system as
            <b>Authorized</b>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-inbox me-1"></i> Intake — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can:</b> submit or create an intake, attach documents, and let AI pre-fill it.
          <b>You should:</b> capture the system's purpose, classification drivers and contacts accurately —
          this seeds the whole assessment. <b>When:</b> at the very start, before any project or assessment
          exists. Accept the intake to move it into a project.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-folder2-open me-1"></i> Project — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can:</b> set the ITSG-33 categorization (C/I/A), security profile, project branding and
          documents, and start assessments. <b>You should:</b> confirm the classification is right before
          starting the assessment (it drives the control baseline). <b>When:</b> right after accepting the
          intake, and whenever the system's scope changes.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-clipboard-check me-1"></i> Assessment &amp; tailoring — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can:</b> pick the framework/profile, tailor applicability / inheritance / priority, manage
          controls, and generate suggested-evidence drafts. <b>You should:</b> remove controls that don't apply
          and mark inherited ones, so the provider only answers what's relevant. <b>When:</b> before you assign
          the assessment for evidence.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-paperclip me-1"></i> Evidence gathering — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can (provider):</b> write evidence, generate/refine drafts with AI, attach artifacts, mark
          controls Ready, revert from history, filter by status and act in bulk. <b>You should:</b> replace
          every placeholder with real values and attach the requested artifacts before marking Ready, then
          Submit. <b>When:</b> after you're assigned the assessment, until you submit. An assessor can
          <b>Take ownership</b> to edit and must re-assign to hand it back.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-search me-1"></i> Audit &amp; review — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can (assessor):</b> score each control's evidence strength, leave feedback, set review
          statuses, filter/bulk-act, and return the assessment for revision. <b>You should:</b> base the
          determination on evidence you can actually verify; flag thin evidence for re-submission rather than
          passing it. <b>When:</b> after the provider submits and you Start audit, through to Complete audit.</p>

        <h3><i class="bi bi-award me-1"></i> Decision &amp; authorization — what, and when</h3>
        <p><b>You can:</b> create a decision package that pins an assessment version, capture POA&amp;M
          conditions, record signatures/expiry, and export the authorization. <b>You should:</b> attach POA&amp;M
          items for any residual risk on an iATO and set a realistic expiry. <b>When:</b> once the audit is
          complete and a result is set.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="projects" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-folder2-open"></i> {{t 'hf.sProjects'}}</h2>
        <p>A <b>project</b> is the system under assessment. Create one from <b>Projects → New Project</b>, or
          from an <b>intake</b> — the short profile a client submits (classification, C/I/A, PII, hosting,
          technologies, contacts). Accepting an intake creates or updates its project.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Documents</b> — upload the system security plan, TRA, test results and provider attestations
            on the project page; they're available to the assessment and listed on the project report.</li>
          <li><b>Report branding</b> — set an organization name, logo, colours and footer on the project's
            <b>Report branding</b> card. Branding resolves <i>project → organization → platform default</i>,
            so a project can override the org defaults (see <a href="?section=settings">Organization settings</a>).</li>
          <li><b>Archive / delete</b> — archive hides a project from lists (reversible); delete requires typing
            the exact name and purges it with its assessments.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="assessments" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-clipboard-check"></i> {{t 'hf.sAssessments'}}</h2>
        <p>An <b>assessment</b> is the SA&amp;A record for a system: a tailored control set, evidence, audit
          results and the authorization decision. Create one from the project page, choose the framework and
          profile (e.g. ITSG-33 PBMM), then <b>Tailor</b> the controls — mark applicability, inheritance and
          priority.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Versioning</b> — assessments are append-only. Each meaningful change creates a new version
            you can view or revert to (reverting creates a <i>new</i> version, never overwriting one), giving
            a full audit trail.</li>
          <li><b>Control families</b> — the assessment groups controls by family; the assessment report scores
            posture per family.</li>
          <li><b>Assign &amp; send</b> — assigning an assessment to a project user activates it and sends the
            invite in one step (the "Assign &amp; send" action). The assignee is the current owner of the
            evidence; the assessor can <b>Take ownership</b> to edit and must re-assign to hand it back.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="mt-3">Reviewing evidence (audit)</h3>
        <p>When the assessment is in the <b>audit</b> phase, each control shows the submitted evidence plus a
          review panel:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Evidence scoring</b> — rate the evidence's <b>reliability</b> (self-attested → assessor-tested),
            its <b>sufficiency</b>, and the control's <b>impact weight</b>. These roll up to a weighted
            evidence-strength score with a weak-evidence flag. The score <i>supports</i> your determination
            (satisfied / partial / not satisfied) and the ATO/iATO decision — it never replaces your judgement.</li>
          <li><b>Review status</b> — mark each control <b>Reviewed</b>, <b>Requires re-submission</b>, or
            <b>Removed</b>, and leave feedback for the provider.</li>
          <li><b>Filter &amp; bulk</b> — filter the control list by status (choose several at once), select
            controls (or Select all), and apply a bulk action from the floating bar or the right-click menu
            (Accept / Requires re-submission / Remove).</li>
          <li><b>Return for revision</b> — "Return for revision &amp; re-assign" reopens the assessment and
            re-sends the invite so the provider fixes only the controls flagged <i>Requires re-submission</i>;
            those unlock for editing and are re-set to Ready once new evidence is provided.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="evidence" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-paperclip"></i> {{t 'hf.sEvidence'}}</h2>
        <p>For each control, the system owner records <b>evidence</b> (text + attachments) and the assessor
          records an <b>audit result</b> (satisfied / partially satisfied / not satisfied) with comments.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>AI evidence guidance</b> — from an assessment you can ask the Aegis SA Assistant to draft
            evidence guidance from the uploaded documentation. You review a preview before anything is saved;
            token usage is metered against your plan unless you bring your own AI provider.</li>
          <li><b>Suggest a draft</b> — on any control, "Suggest a draft" pre-fills a starting-point answer with
            <code>[[VALUE: …]]</code> placeholders for the specifics you supply and <code>[[ATTACH: …]]</code>
            for artifacts to upload. A draft is clearly marked and does <i>not</i> count as provided evidence
            until you edit it. The evidence assistant can also propose drafts you <b>Approve</b> / <b>Approve
            all</b> straight into the fields.</li>
          <li><b>Suggest drafts in bulk</b> — the bar at the top runs a background job that drafts every control
            with <i>no evidence yet</i> (it never touches ones you've filled). It keeps running if you leave the
            page, shows live progress, and reports any that were skipped.</li>
          <li><b>Mark Ready</b> — lock a control once its evidence is complete; <b>Reactivate</b> to edit again.
            Ready controls are protected from bulk/AI overwrites.</li>
          <li><b>Edited by &amp; history</b> — each control shows who last edited it (including <i>Aegis AI</i>),
            and a per-control <b>History</b> keeps every save, draft, Ready and reactivate so you can <b>Revert</b>
            to an earlier version.</li>
          <li><b>Filter &amp; bulk</b> — filter controls by status (Pending / Draft / Provided / Ready) and act on
            a selection in bulk (Mark Ready / Reactivate / Suggest a draft) from the floating bar or right-click.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="decisions" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-award"></i> {{t 'hf.sDecisions'}}</h2>
        <p>Authorization lives in a <b>decision package</b> (the iATO / ATO). A package <b>pins</b> a specific
          assessment version, so the decision always reflects exactly what was authorized — even as work
          continues on later assessment versions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Draft the executive summary, residual-risk statement, conditions and rationale; capture the
            authorizing official, assessor and expiry; then advance the package through its states
            (recommended → decided → issued). An issued package is sealed.</li>
          <li>Editorial fields are versioned with an audit trail; a package can be reverted like an assessment
            (until it's issued).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="poam" class="help-sec">
        <h2><i class="bi bi-list-check"></i> {{t 'hf.sPoam'}}</h2>
        <p>A <b>Plan of Action &amp; Milestones (POA&amp;M)</b> item is a condition on an interim authorization:
          the work required before a full ATO. POA&amp;M items belong to the <b>decision package</b>, not the
          assessment.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Each item runs a small review loop — the team submits evidence; the assessor accepts, rejects
            (with feedback) or defers it.</li>
          <li><b>Promotion to full ATO is blocked</b> while any item is overdue, deferred or outstanding.
            Deadline changes keep a history (original date, every change, and the reason).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="reports" class="help-sec">
        <span class="new-badge">{{t 'hf.new'}}</span>
        <h2><i class="bi bi-file-earmark-bar-graph"></i> {{t 'hf.sReports'}}</h2>
        <p>Every record has a built-in, <b>deterministic</b> report — a straight render of the record, no AI.
          Use the <b>Export</b> button on an intake, assessment, project, decision package or POA&amp;M page:
          it opens a quick menu to download <b>PDF, Word, HTML or Markdown</b> (plus <b>CSV</b> where the data
          is tabular), or <b>Open full report</b> for the on-screen viewer.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Reports hub</b> (<i>Reports</i> in the menu) lists everything you can report on, plus a
            <b>Report templates</b> catalog of the six built-in report types and an org <b>Portfolio</b>
            summary (admins).</li>
          <li><b>Branding</b> — reports use the resolved project/org branding (logo, colours, footer,
            classification banner).</li>
          <li><b>Reproducible</b> — decision-package and POA&amp;M reports render the pinned assessment
            version, so they reproduce exactly.</li>
          <li>Custom, AI-generated reports are on the roadmap; today's reports never use AI.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="settings" class="help-sec">
        <span class="new-badge">{{t 'hf.new'}}</span>
        <h2><i class="bi bi-sliders"></i> {{t 'hf.sSettings'}}</h2>
        <p>Root administrators manage tenant-wide settings under <b>Admin → Organization settings</b>:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Bring your own integrations</b> — your own <b>SMTP</b> mail server, <b>SMS</b> provider and
            a <b>custom domain</b>, instead of the platform defaults. Each has a real validation check (DNS
            lookup, SMTP/SMS connection) with a last-verified timestamp and a 24-hour log.</li>
          <li><b>AI provider</b> — use the built-in AI (metered against your plan) or bring your own
            frontier-model API (Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI, Google) or a custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint. With
            your own provider you pay that provider directly and no platform tokens are consumed.</li>
          <li><b>Report branding</b> — org-level logo, colours and footer inherited by every project.</li>
          <li><b>Notifications</b> — control mention notifications (in-app immediate; email digests are
            link-only by default).</li>
          <li><b>Security</b> — tenant secrets (SMTP password, SMS token, AI keys) are encrypted at rest.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="navigation" class="help-sec">
        <span class="new-badge">{{t 'hf.new'}}</span>
        <h2><i class="bi bi-layout-sidebar"></i> {{t 'hf.sNavigation'}}</h2>
        <p>The menu is <b>dockable per user</b>. Use the menu-settings control (the <i class="bi bi-sliders"></i>
          button in the breadcrumb bar when the menu is on top, or the cluster at the bottom of the rail when
          it's on the side) to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Position</b> — dock the menu <b>top, left or right</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Pin</b> — keep it always visible, or unpin to let it auto-hide (reveal it by moving to the
            docked edge).</li>
          <li><b>Set as default</b> (the star) — save your current position + pin as your default for every
            sign-in. The star lights up when the current view matches your saved default.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <b>breadcrumb</b> under the menu always shows where you are (Home › Section › Record).</p>
      </section>

